<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="fb5648d2">&lt;p&gt;
    Common considerations when developing a project schedule include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Activity dependency types
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Leads and Lags
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Resource Leveling&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Schedule compression
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Critical path method (CPM)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Risk analysis (or what-if scenario analysis)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Activity dependency types
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Finish-to-start (FS)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This dependency indicates that the finish date of the predecessor task determines the start date of the successor
    tasks.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Start-to-start (SS)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This dependency indicates that the start date of the predecessor task determines the start date of the successor
    tasks.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Finish-to-finish (FF)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This dependency indicates that the finish date of the predecessor task determines the finish date of the successor
    task.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Start-to-finish (SF)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This dependency indicates that the start date of the predecessor task determines the finish date of the successor task.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Leads and Lags
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Here is a simple illustration of adding a lead:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_small.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/leads_lags.png&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;371&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Resource Leveling
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Here is a simple example&amp;nbsp;that levels overallocated resources:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_small.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/res_leveling.png&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;411&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Schedule Compression
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Schedule compression is used to shorten project schedule without changing project scope.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Two main techniques are used:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Crashing
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Fast tracking
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Critical Path Method
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The critical path (CP) is the longest duration path through a network of project activities that has no “float” or
    “slack,” i.e., any delay to the activities on this path delays the entire project.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The critical path therefore determines the minimum time required to complete the project.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        “Critical” does not refer to the level of importance of these tasks but to how their scheduling will affect the
        project’s finish date.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Risk or &quot;What If&quot; Scenario Analysis
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Risk&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The potential that a chosen action or activity (including the choice of inaction) will lead to a loss (an undesirable
    outcome).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Assumption&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A proposition that is taken for granted, as if it were true based upon presupposition without preponderance of the
    facts.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &amp;nbsp;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
